TITLE:: BusManager
summary:: buses with names, and a window to see them in
categories:: Libraries>BusManager
related:: Classes/BusManagerEntry, Classes/BusManagerGUI, Classes/Bus

DESCRIPTION::
A registry of buses under names, so a patch can say where it is sending sound
instead of counting bus indices - and a window that shows what is in each of
them.

code::
s.boot;
BusManager.add(\reverb, 2);
BusManager.gui;

{ Out.ar(BusManager.index(\reverb), PinkNoise.ar(0.1) ! 2) }.play;
::

Everything is on the class: there is one registry per session, the way there is
one server, and no instance to keep hold of.

It depends on nothing. When strong::ModuleCommon:: is installed alongside
it, OneShot, LongSample and AdaptiveTextures accept a name wherever they take a
bus, and a module set to a name follows it - see link::#Modules:: below.

subsection:: Who owns the bus

Three ways of putting a name in the registry, and which one you use decides who
is allowed to free the bus behind it. This is the one thing the extension has to
get right, so it is worth knowing which is which.

table::
## strong::kind:: || strong::made by:: || strong::freed on remove:: || strong::taken again after a boot::
## code::\owned:: || code::add:: || yes || yes
## code::\fixed:: || code::addIndex:: || no || no
## code::\external:: || code::addBus:: || no || no
::

code::
BusManager.add(\reverb, 2);                     // the registry allocates it
BusManager.addIndex(\desk, 8, 2);               // a bare index
BusManager.addBus(\drums, someModule.tapBus);   // somebody else's Bus
::

subsection:: Metering and listening

Metering is one small synth per bus replying fifteen times a second. It is off
until something asks for it: opening the window turns it on, closing it turns it
off again.

code::
BusManager.meter_(true);
BusManager.level(\reverb);          // the loudest channel right now
BusManager.at(\reverb).levels;      // one per channel

BusManager.listen(\reverb);         // hear it on the monitor output
BusManager.listenOff;
::

One bus is listened to at a time. Listening to a bus that overlaps the monitor
output is refused rather than played, since that is a feedback loop.

subsection:: Modules

code::
m = OneShot(\drums, 16, outBus: \reverb);
m.outBus = \out3_4;
AdaptiveTextures(\tex, listen: \drums, out: \reverb);
::

Every bus field in those windows also grows a dropdown of the registry next to
its number box - see link::Classes/ModuleBusMenu:: - so a bus can be picked by
name rather than typed as a number.

A module set to a name follows that name: when the bus behind it moves - which
it does when the server reboots - the module goes with it. Setting the field to
a plain index unbinds it again, and a name nothing knows changes nothing at all.
Presets save the index and the name both, and restore the name when the registry
knows it.


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: add
Allocate a bus and keep it under a name. Re-using a name replaces what was
there, freeing it first.
ARGUMENT:: name
a Symbol.
ARGUMENT:: channels
how many channels. 2 by default.
ARGUMENT:: rate
code::\audio:: or code::\control::. code::\audio:: by default.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/BusManagerEntry::, or nil if the server had no buses left.

METHOD:: addIndex
Name a bare index the registry did not allocate - hardware channels, or a bus
you are keeping track of yourself. Never freed by the registry.

METHOD:: addBus
Name a link::Classes/Bus:: that belongs to something else: a module's tapBus, a
SuperDirt orbit, an Ndef's bus. Never freed and never re-allocated.

METHOD:: addHardware
Seed the ins and outs of the current audio device as stereo pairs -
code::\out1_2::, code::\out3_4::, code::\in1_2:: and so on. Done once the first
time the registry is used; call it again after changing device.

METHOD:: remove
Take a name out. An owned bus is freed. Modules still following the name are
named in a warning rather than stopped.

METHOD:: rename
Give a bus a different name. Refused if the new name is taken.

METHOD:: clear
Empty the registry: frees what it owns, lets go of what it does not.

METHOD:: at
The link::Classes/BusManagerEntry:: under a name, or nil.

METHOD:: bus
The link::Classes/Bus:: under a name, or nil.

METHOD:: index
The bus index under a name, or nil. This is what a routing argument wants.

METHOD:: includes
Whether a name is in the registry.

METHOD:: names
Every name, in the order they were added.

METHOD:: size
How many buses are registered.

METHOD:: level
The loudest channel of a bus right now. 0 unless metering is on.

METHOD:: meter
Whether the meters are running. Off by default; the window turns it on while it
is open.

METHOD:: listen
Route one bus to the monitor output so you can hear it. Starting another stops
the first. Refused for a control bus, and for a bus overlapping the monitor
output.

METHOD:: listenOff
Stop listening.

METHOD:: isListeningTo
Whether that name is the one being listened to.

METHOD:: monitoring
The entry being listened to, or nil.

METHOD:: monitorOut
Where listening goes. 0 by default. Moving it onto the bus being listened to
stops the monitor rather than making a loop.

METHOD:: monitorAmp
Monitor level, 0 to 2. 0.5 by default.

METHOD:: saveLayout
METHOD:: loadLayout
The set of declared buses, written to one code::.scd:: file and read back. A
bare name lands in the repository's code::layouts:: folder. Loading replaces
owned and fixed entries and leaves external ones alone. Nothing is ever restored
automatically.

METHOD:: layouts
The layout files on disk, newest first - what the window's dropdown offers.

METHOD:: layoutDir
Where they live.

METHOD:: entries
Every link::Classes/BusManagerEntry::, in order.

METHOD:: server
The server the registry allocates on. link::Classes/Server::.default unless set.
Setting it empties the registry.

METHOD:: gui
Open the window, or bring it to the front. link::Classes/BusManagerGUI::.

METHOD:: meterRate
How many times a second a meter reports. 15.


EXAMPLES::

code::
s.boot;

// a bus, and a window to watch it in
BusManager.add(\reverb, 2);
BusManager.gui;

// something to look at
~noise = { Out.ar(BusManager.index(\reverb), PinkNoise.ar(0.1) ! 2) }.play;

BusManager.level(\reverb);      // moving
BusManager.listen(\reverb);     // and now audible
BusManager.listenOff;
~noise.free;

// naming a bus somebody else made: never freed by the registry
BusManager.addBus(\borrowed, Bus.audio(s, 2));

// the interface is already in there
BusManager.names;

// save the lot, and get it back
BusManager.saveLayout("tonight");
BusManager.clear;
BusManager.loadLayout("tonight");
::

There is a longer walkthrough in code::examples/bus-manager-demo.scd:: in the
repository.
